{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-2 overshoot audit on ruling 9753818f, read at main a200cd0a. I cannot justify the proposed largest-rule-row number as entryBytes plus usesPerEntry times entryBytes. The fact portion alone has the conservative payload term 128 × 262,144 = 33,554,432 bytes, before JSON separators. The row also includes whole subjects, not only their bounded slots (derive/src/guards.ts:133-137).\n\nEach subject includes Item.attributed, the history of every author and principal (contract/src/read.ts:54-69). withMembers appends every new reference without a count check (derive/src/attribution.ts:16-19); changeItem feeds that history from author slots and principals (derive/src/item-effects.ts:44-69). partyMembers constrains the current party list, not attributed (derive/src/effects.ts:295). Floors preserve items while countedEntries drops the archived prefix (derive/src/floor.ts:21-24,90-96). Thus these row parts have no definition-local byte ceiling. The current production floor offer is disabled except for fixtureArchive (scope/src/object-archive.ts:26), so this is not a claim that production already grows that history through floors.\n\nA separate shipped-platform fact: a read-only parse of every canonical declaration in platform/src/native-declarations.generated.ts finds zero guard keys named rule. These declarations use platform code marks. Therefore a nonzero generic rule-row maximum multiplied by a platform-only pending count mixes different producer sets. This audit ran no build or test and changed no source.\n\nPlanner question: should the faults note state a bound only for today's no-floor production deployment, and derive subject history from the whole journal budget, or state that no independent rule-row constant follows from the accepted definition bounds? Please also name whether the pending count covers only native platform scopes or declared scopes they install. A finite overshoot product needs those two scopes to agree.","body":{"stale_bases